DTrace eighth expansion: categories, wait outcomes, base file operations
======================================================================

Result and scope
----------------
The requested 20 focused implementation passes plus three census-discovered
filesystem/device gaps add 45 probe contracts: 30 kernel SDT and 15 USDT.
Ten bsdinstruments profiles join the automatic base-install profile catalog.
The earlier coverage rounds remain useful; this round fills missing semantic
boundaries instead of adding duplicate entry/return probes to every function.
coverage-eighth.json records exact selectors, argument types, categories,
priority, source hashes, and the five existing block-probe dependencies.

The whole-tree census covers 55,266 C/C++/assembly/header/provider files in
1,243 groups under sys, bin, sbin, lib, libexec, usr.bin, usr.sbin, cddl,
crypto, contrib, stand and rescue. It records 646 files with known probe-macro
evidence. The narrower kernel census covers 13,806 files in 403 groups. These
are source inventories, not claims of semantic completeness, compilation,
installation, or live tracing. Imported/optional code is included. Dynamic
syscall, FBT and lockstat coverage is not represented by macro counts.

Whole-tree findings are retained in whole-tree-census.json, including groups
with no detected macros. Autofs, CUSE and p9fs had no matching SDT sites in the
initial kernel census and now have explicit request/command boundaries.
Architecture/bootstrap code, non-amd64 targets, dormant modules and every
individual hardware driver's runtime behavior are not validated by this run.

Category and priority policy
----------------------------
P0 lifecycle/flow: paired syscall work, filesystem service requests, child
completion, and teardown boundaries. Existing bhyve migration/snapshot,
Bluetooth/Mesh, MACF and descriptor contracts are retained from prior rounds.
P0 errors: raw sleep return, syscall errno, daemon/transport outcome, retry,
peer loss and tag exhaustion. A wake is not necessarily operation success.
P0 latency: consumers compute elapsed time from start/done or block/wake;
probes introduce no clocks, duration arithmetic, or slow-path thresholds.
P1 resource: queue occupancy, swap-buffer scarcity, racct throttling and 9P
transaction-tag exhaustion. Allocation internals are not newly instrumented.
P1 synchronization: contended pipe lock, kernel waits, reference/task drains,
USB FIFO, AIO, and UNIX peer-buffer backpressure, rather than every lock hit.
P1 I/O/communication: GEOM, USB, filesystem requests, durability, file mutation,
libfetch readiness and IPC. Only metadata and scalar outcomes are recorded.
P2 security: chmod reports requested mode and actual syscall result, including
errors suppressed by -f. It is not a replacement for kernel MACF policy
checks or an authentication audit. Existing MACF/security contracts remain.
P2 counters/state: profiles aggregate events and depth distributions; no new
shared kernel counters, atomics, health model or deadlock detector is added.

Focused implementation passes
-----------------------------
 1. POSIX message queues: wake outcomes and queue depth complement existing block probes.
 2. System V message queues: distinguish periodic retry wakes from interruption and receive wakes.
 3. System V semaphores: raw timed wait completion before object revalidation.
 4. Pipe serialization: lock contention latency and interruption.
 5. AIO suspend: per-sleep latency and raw wake status.
 6. Filesystem unmount: reference drain timing outside filesystem callbacks.
 7. USB FIFO: process-context I/O waits including device-loss wake status.
 8. Swap pager: async write-buffer exhaustion wait and available capacity on wake.
 9. Resource accounting: throttling delay and state on wake, before timeout clearing.
10. GEOM control: queued event execution latency and handler outcome.
11. UNIX stream sockets: peer receive-buffer backpressure latency and wake errors.
12. Kqueue teardown: reference and taskqueue drain timing with explicit reason.
13. dd: fsync/fdatasync latency and durable-write failure.
14. cp: interrupted transfer retries with descriptor identities, no paths.
15. libfetch: connect/read/write readiness wait latency, poll timeout, errno, revents.
16. rm/unlink: file/directory removal attempts, latency and errno.
17. chmod: requested permission mode, actual syscall outcome including suppressed errors.
18. mkdir: each parent/leaf creation attempt, requested mode and errno.
19. truncate: resize and hole-deallocation extent, latency and failure.
20. mv: parent-only copy/remove child wait latency, raw exit status and wait error.
21. Autofs: joined/new request wait including daemon status and interruption before releasing request reference.
22. CUSE: client command completion and outstanding process-reference drain, including server closing.
23. 9P: whole synchronous RPC through preparation/transport/reply error and exhausted transaction tags.

Kernel contracts and limits
---------------------------
mqueue send-wake/receive-wake(instance, raw_error, current_messages) pair with
existing send-block/receive-block. One wake per sleep. EAGAIN is recorded
before the implementation maps it to ETIMEDOUT; a concurrently available
message/slot can still cause the overall request to succeed. Depth is read
under the queue mutex after decrementing the waiter count.
sysvmsg msgsnd-wake/msgrcv-wake(msqid, raw_error) pair with existing block
sites. Send uses periodic hz sleeps, so EWOULDBLOCK often means retry, not
terminal failure. sysvsem semop-wake(semid, raw_error) precedes semaphore
revalidation; it does not certify the object still exists. Neither family
newly dereferences an IPC object after sleeping.
pipe lock-wait-start(pipe)/done(pipe,error) measures a single serialization
sleep, not the full pipe read/write. aio suspend-wait-start(process)/done
(process,error) records one sleep before ERESTART-to-EINTR normalization.
mount drain-start(mount)/done(mount,error) surrounds the unmount reference
drain. kqueue drain-start/done(queue,reason) uses reason 0 for outstanding
references and 1 for scheduled task drain. Its second argument is not errno.
swap buffer-wait-start/done(available_async_buffers) surrounds the blocked
pageout buffer wait. racct throttle-start/done(process,throttle_ticks) reports
state before the sleep and before post-wake timeout clearing. Negative ticks
mean indefinite throttling. Done does not assert a resource was acquired.
usb fifo-wait-start(fifo)/done(fifo,error) wraps process-context FIFO sleep;
post-wake device loss reports EIO. Already-failed devices return before the
pair. The declarations/sites are absent with USB_GLOBAL_INCLUDE_FILE, and
UGEN-disabled builds omit them. No new USB interrupt or boot-time site exists.
geom control-start(request)/done(request,handler_error) wraps the queued GEOM
event. Validation/copyin precedes it and final copyout follows it; the result
is not necessarily the final ioctl errno.
unpcb send-wait-start(socket)/done(socket,error) observes waiting for space
in the peer receive buffer. The socket argument is the peer buffer owner.
autofs request-start(id)/done(id,wait_error,request_error) runs after a new or
joined request is referenced, through waiting, before release. Multiple threads
may share the id, so profiles pair by thread. An interrupted wait can leave an
unfinished daemon request; request_error alone is insufficient to infer success.
cuse command-start(command,input_length)/done(command,error) includes reply
wait and draining process references. Error is the existing CUSE return domain,
not necessarily a positive errno. Server closing is included. Payloads are not.
p9fs request-start(client,type)/done(client,type,error) covers preparation,
transport request and generic reply validation, including preparation failure.
Operation-specific payload decoding in the caller happens later. tag-exhausted
(client) reports alloc_unr failure before returning P9_NOTAG. No paths, user
identities, protocol payloads or credentials are exposed.

Userland contracts and installation
----------------------------------
ddio sync-start(fd,data_only)/done(fd,data_only,result,errno): fsync is 0,
fdatasync is 1. errno is zero on success. This is the actual syscall duration;
it does not promise storage hardware semantics beyond that syscall.
cpcopy retry(source_fd,destination_fd,errno) reports the existing EINTR retry
branch. Non-EINTR failure and fallback retain earlier contracts.
fetchio poll-start(fd,requested_events,timeout_ms)/done(fd,returned_events,
result,errno) wraps connect/read/write readiness polling. Result 0 is timeout,
negative is error; returned_events is normalized to zero on a negative result
instead of reading an unspecified field. The library still owns retry policy.
removeio operation-start(kind)/done(kind,result,errno): kind 0 unlink, 1 rmdir.
Recursive and nonrecursive paths, and the unlink alias, share these sites.
Pre-stat rejection, confirmation refusal and whiteout undelete are outside them.
modechange operation-start(mode)/done(mode,result,errno) surrounds fchmodat.
Requested mode is not an authoritative record of final ACL permissions.
dircreate operation-start(mode)/done(mode,result,errno) surrounds mkdir for
parents and leaves. Existing-parent EEXIST can subsequently be accepted by -p;
post-creation chmod failures are outside this pair.
resizeio operation-start(fd,kind,offset,length)/done(fd,kind,result,errno): kind
0 ftruncate (offset=0,length=new_size), kind 1 fspacectl deallocation extent.
Open/stat/argument rejection happens before these sites.
moveio child-start(pid)/done(requested_pid,waitpid_result,raw_wait_status,errno)
is parent-only, outside vfork children, for the cp/rm fallback helpers. On
wait failure status is zero, with errno separate; decode a successful wait
status with the usual WIFEXITED/WEXITSTATUS/WIFSIGNALED conventions.

New rm/chmod/mkdir/truncate providers follow MK_DTRACE; no provider sources or
calls are required in MK_DTRACE=no builds. Existing dd/cp/mv/libfetch integration
is extended. Normal base targets install the utilities and libfetch; the rm
unlink alias uses the same executable. No packages or live filesystem files
were installed by this task. Ten profiles are selected automatically by the
bsdinstruments Makefile, with 355 total catalog entries at validation time:
  ipc-wake-results
  kernel-drain-waits
  resource-pressure-waits
  device-control-waits
  unix-peer-pressure
  base-file-mutations
  copy-durability
  fetch-readiness
  move-child-waits
  userspace-filesystem-requests

Safety and overhead
-------------------
Kernel sites use existing SDT disabled NOPs and compile away without
KDTRACE_HOOKS. No new allocations, locks, timestamp calls, buffer copies or
logging are added to kernel probe sites. Most new sites run only when a wait
or relatively coarse operation occurs. USDT arguments are guarded by ENABLED
checks; wrappers restore errno. A fake provider that deliberately overwrites
errno verifies every new USDT wrapper in enabled/disabled builds. This is a
wrapper test, not a live DTrace firing test.

Pointers are opaque identities. Profiles never save and dereference objects.
There are no new sites in DTrace internals, early provider/bootstrap setup,
NMI/IPI entry, CPU restore internals, pmap or allocation fast paths. Prior
IRQ-thread and VM-object coverage retains its documented scope. These probes
cannot reveal events occurring before DTrace providers have been registered.
Latency includes scheduler/preemption delay. Start tracing before the workload;
dropped records invalidate pairing. Per-thread nesting handles synchronous
calls; PID filters refer to executing context, not always the originating app.

