Existing shell ATF suites are run against built utilities via PATH wrappers.
Only those utility processes use the matching prepared-world libraries; host
ATF tools retain their installed libraries. This matters because the source
world requires newer fts symbol versions than the running host. Cases run in
fresh temporary directories. Root/mount fixtures are explicitly skipped;
other skips and expected failures retain the test suite's result and reason.
The four expected failures are chmod flag/verbose cases in both modes on this
host filesystem. They are not reported as passing assertions.

The 22 smoke records cover each new USDT header's guard/errno behavior in both
modes (16), libfetch local socketpair timeout/interruption/read/write/EOF (2),
utility success/error and fsync/fdatasync/pipe-failure behavior (2), and mv
cross-filesystem directory copy/remove helper waits (2). They perform no
external network access, module loading, daemon startup or mounts.

